{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018GvJDJrC9ea511WRVHBVqr","type":"tool_result","content":"# src/engine/\n\nThe torrent engine and its transport (ADR-012). Phase 3 moves the\nengine here from `src/renderer/webtorrent.js` and turns it into a Node\nsidecar; US-039 landed the transport seam first and US-040 added the\nsidecar entry point.\n\n**There is exactly one engine.** US-044 moved the UI onto the sidecar\nand US-045 deleted the hidden window, the engine that ran in it and the\n`ipcMain` relay between them. Engine behaviour has a single home now:\n`src/engine/index.js`.\n\n`build:main` compiles the whole of `src` except the four Vite-owned\npaths, so a file added here is emitted to `build/engine/` with no build\nchange -- that is also the path unit tests require.\n\n## The bus contract\n\nThe engine talks to the UI through an injected bus and never names a\ntransport API itself. A bus is exactly two functions:\n\n- `send(channel, ...args)` -- deliver one message to the UI.\n- `on(channel, handler)` -- subscribe; resolves to an unsubscribe\n  function. The handler receives the protocol's argument list only,\n  never the transport's own event object. Stripping that object is the\n  bus's job, not the caller's.\n\nThe bus carries no protocol. Channel names, argument lists and payload\nshapes live in the command and event tables in\n`docs/platform-shim.md`; the codec that turns them into bytes is\n`src/shared/wire.js`. A bus implementation that knows a channel name is\na bug.\n\nThe engine constructs its bus in exactly one place and\n`test/unit/engine-entry.js` asserts that, by scanning the engine source\nfor transport APIs, for stray `.send(` calls and for subscriptions that\nbypass `bus.on`. That scan is what keeps the seam substitutable, so keep\nit passing rather than relaxing it.\n\n`bus-electron.js`, the Phase 3 transitional implementation, is gone:\nUS-044 pointed the UI at `bus-websocket.js` and US-045 deleted the\nhidden window it depended on, along with its unit test.\n\nThis directory is outside `src/renderer/`, so the renderer's Node bans\ndo not apply: the sidecar is a Node process and uses `fs`, `crypto`\nand friends directly. Since US-046 both boundary mechanisms cover it\nfor the **opposite** mistake instead. `eslint-boundary.config.js` has\nan `src/engine/**` override banning `electron`, `@electron/remote` and\n`original-fs` plus every DOM global, and `npm run lint:boundary` lints\nthis tree as well as the renderer;\n`test/unit/platform-boundary.js` scans the same two things in source,\nwhich is what catches the `require()` spellings ESLint cannot see.\n\n## `bus-websocket.js`\n\nThe sidecar implementation, and the one that survives Phase 4. It binds\na `ws` server to `127.0.0.1` on port 0, encodes every frame with\n`src/shared/wire.js`, and exposes `send`/`on` plus three lifecycle\nfunctions the entry point needs: `listen()` (resolves to the bound\nport, idempotent), `address()` and `close()`.\n\nThree behaviours are contract, not incidental:\n\n- **A send with nobody connected is dropped, not queued.** Commands\n  always originate from the UI, so a connected client is the normal\n  case; the pre-connect queue that ADR-012 requires lives on the *UI*\n  side, in `ipc-engine.js`, and has since US-044.\n- **A malformed frame and a throwing handler are both reported through\n  `onError`, never rethrown and never fatal to the socket.** The engine\n  is a long-lived process; one bad message from a buggy UI must not end\n  the session. `index.js` wires `onError` to the same\n  `wt-uncaught-error` path the process handlers use.\n- **The token, when configured, is checked in `verifyClient`**, which\n  answers a bad upgrade with HTTP 401 before any frame is read. It is\n  read from `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_TOKEN` by `index.js`. No token means an\n  open server, which is only acceptable for running the sidecar by\n  hand. The `Origin` check ADR-012 also asks for is still not\n  implemented -- US-044 connected the UI without needing it, so it needs\n  the real header the Electron and Tauri webviews send and is US-046\n  territory.\n\n## `index.js`\n\nThe sidecar entry point: `node build/engine/index.js`. It imports no\nElectron module and uses no DOM.\n\n**The stdout contract with the host.** Exactly one line matches\n`WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PORT=<port>`, printed once the server is listening.\nThe host end is `src-tauri/src/engine.rs`, which holds the same prefix\nin `PORT_LINE_PREFIX`; keep the prefix and the `=` if you touch either\nside. Every other line on stdout and stderr is pumped through to the\nhost's own output under an `Engine: ` prefix. `src/main/` is gone, so\nthe Electron reader that older plans name here\n(`src/main/engine-process.js`, US-041) went with it.\n\nDifferences from the hidden-window engine, all deliberate:\n\n- No `ipcReadyWebTorrent`. Readiness is the port line plus an accepted\n  socket.\n- No `wt-generate-torrent-poster` and no `wt-poster`. Both needed a DOM;\n  US-043 deleted them from the wire and poster generation now runs in the\n  UI renderer (`src/renderer/lib/torrent-poster.js`, ADR-013). The engine\n  handles no media at all -- do not add a media dependency back to it.\n- No `window.client` and no `window.testOfflineMode`.\n- Uncaught errors come from `process.on('uncaughtException')` and\n  `process.on('unhandledRejection')` rather than a `window` error\n  listener, and still go out on `wt-uncaught-error`.\n- `SIGTERM` and `SIGINT` destroy the client and close every streaming\n  server, so a quit does not leave the download directory held open.\n- With `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PARENT_PIPE` set, stdin ending shuts the\n  process down the same way. That is the orphan backstop for a host\n  that dies without running its `before-quit`, and it is gated behind\n  the flag because a hand-run `node build/engine/index.js` gets\n  `/dev/null` on stdin, which ends immediately.\n- `getTorrent()` throws a plain `Error` with the same message\n  `TorrentKeyNotFoundError` produced. The class lives in\n  `src/renderer/lib/errors.js`, only its message ever crossed the wire,\n  and the sidecar does not import renderer code.\n\nThe streaming servers are a `Map` keyed by info hash, and each entry\ncounts its holders (US-043): `wt-start-server` is idempotent per torrent\nand always replies, `wt-stop-server` takes the info hash to release, and\nthe server dies with its last holder. Poster generation and playback can\nhold servers for different torrents at once, so a single module-level\n`server` is not enough any more.\n\n`config.TORRENT_PATH` and `config.POSTER_PATH` resolve under plain\n`node` because US-040 made `src/config.js` require `electron` lazily,\ninside the main-process branch of `getPath()`. Do not hoist that back\nto a top-level import: the packaged sidecar has no\n`node_modules/electron` to resolve.\n\nThe host runs the sidecar as **Electron in Node mode**\n(`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1`), not as a separate `node` binary, so that the\npackaged app needs no second runtime. In that mode\n`process.versions.electron` is set but `process.type` is not and\n`require('electron')` has no `app`, which is why `getPath()` in\n`src/config.js` branches on `process.type` and treats anything that is\nneither `'renderer'` nor `'browser'` as a plain Node process. A check\nfor \"is Electron present\" there breaks the sidecar at load, and only\noutside `NODE_ENV=test` (portable config skips the branch).\n\n## `announce.js`\n\n`announceNow(torrent, dht)` is the discovery half of \"destroy and\nre-add\": a tracker `update()` plus a DHT `lookup()`, with no re-verify\nof pieces on disk and no dropped peer connections. It exists because\nthe natural cadence is slow -- 30 minutes per tracker, 15 minutes plus\njitter for the DHT -- so a torrent whose last peer drops can sit at\nzero peers for half an hour.\n\nThe sweep automates it. Once a minute `index.js` calls\n`runAnnounceSweep`, which re-announces every torrent that has zero\npeers, is not finished and is not paused. Three guards keep it from\nbeing worse than the bug it fixes, and they are the reason the logic\nlives here rather than inline in `index.js`, where it could not be\ntested: a five minute per-torrent cooldown, comfortably above any\ntracker's `min interval`; a cap of five torrents per sweep, served\nleast-recently-announced first so a long list goes round robin; and one\nsummary line per sweep that did anything, carrying the deferred count,\nbecause a silent truncation reads as \"nothing else needed it\".\n\nBe precise about that last one, because the obvious reading is too\nnarrow. The line is not reserved for capped sweeps: a sweep that\nannounces one torrent and defers nothing still logs. What is silent is\na sweep with nothing to do -- `runAnnounceSweep` returns early only\nwhen it selected nothing *and* deferred nothing, so an idle engine\nsays nothing at all.\n\n`index.js` owns only the timer and the `lastAnnounceAt` map. The map\nis seeded when a torrent's info hash arrives -- `client.add()` has just\nannounced `started` and a second announce a minute later would be waste\n-- and cleared in `stopTorrenting`.\n\nThat covers the `wt-stop-torrenting` path, which is the only caller.\nA torrent torn down any other way -- a fatal `torrent` error, say --\nleaves its entry behind. Those orphans are inert rather than a leak:\nthe sweep iterates `client.torrents` and never the map, so an entry\nwith no torrent is never read, and re-adding the same info hash\noverwrites it.\n\nThe three tunables are exported from here, not from `index.js`:\n`SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS`, `COOLDOWN_MS` and `MAX_PER_SWEEP`. `index.js`\nimports the interval rather than repeating `60 * 1000`, so the cadence\nhas one home. `selectStalled` is exported next to `runAnnounceSweep`\nbecause the guards are worth testing without announcing anything.\n\n`test/unit/engine-sweep-wiring.js` pins that wiring by scanning the\n`index.js` source: both imports, `setInterval(runSweep,\nSWEEP_INTERVAL_MS)`, and the seed and the delete on `lastAnnounceAt`.\nAn interval that fires once a minute is too slow to drive from a\nspawned sidecar, and a test-only override of it would be production\ncode that exists for a test -- so renaming `runSweep` or inlining the\nconstant fails that scan instead of going unnoticed.\n  bus.send('wt-server-' + infoHash, info)\n}\n\nfunction stopServer (infoHash) {\n  const entry = servers.get(infoHash)\n  if (!entry) return\n  if (--entry.holders > 0) return\n  servers.delete(infoHash)\n  entry.server.destroy()\n}\n\nfunction stopAllServers () {\n  for (const entry of servers.values()) entry.server.destroy()\n  servers.clear()\n}\n\nconsole.log('Initializing...')\n\nfunction getAudioMetadata (infoHash, index) {\n  const torrent = client.get(infoHash)\n  const file = torrent.files[index]\n\n  // Set initial matadata to display the filename first.\n  const metadata = { title: file.name }\n  bus.send('wt-audio-metadata', infoHash, index, metadata)\n\n  const options = {\n    native: false,\n    skipCovers: true,\n    fileSize: file.length,\n    observer: () => {\n      bus.send('wt-audio-metadata', infoHash, index, {\n        common: metadata.common,\n        format: metadata.format\n      })\n    }\n  }\n  const onMetadata = file.done\n    // If completed; use direct file access\n    ? mm.parseFile(path.join(torrent.path, file.path), options)\n    // otherwise stream\n    : mm.parseStream(file.createReadStream(), file.name, options)\n\n  onMetadata\n    .then(\n      metadata => {\n        bus.send('wt-audio-metadata', infoHash, index, metadata)\n        console.log(`metadata for file='${file.name}' completed.`)\n      },\n      err => {\n        console.log(\n          `error getting audio metadata for ${infoHash}:${index}`,\n          err\n        )\n      }\n    )\n}\n\nfunction selectFiles (torrentOrInfoHash, selections) {\n  // Get the torrent object\n  let torrent\nconst test = require('tape')\nconst fs = require('fs')\nconst path = require('path')\n\n// The banning policy lives in `hashfail.js` and is tested against plain\n// values in `engine-hashfail.js`. What cannot be reached that way is the\n// wiring in `index.js`: driving it for real needs a peer willing to\n// serve corrupt data. So this is a source check, in the same shape as\n// `engine-sweep-wiring.js` -- it asserts the parts are connected, not\n// that they work.\n\nconst ENTRY_SRC = path.join(\n  __dirname, '..', '..', 'src', 'engine', 'index.js')\n\nconst source = fs.readFileSync(ENTRY_SRC, 'utf8')\n\n// Comments discuss all of this at length, so they are stripped first.\n// Every assertion below asserts PRESENCE, which means an unstripped\n// comment body reads as live code and passes -- so block comments are\n// removed whole rather than line by line, for the reason spelled out in\n// `engine-sweep-wiring.js`.\nconst code = source\n  .replace(/\\/\\*[\\s\\S]*?\\*\\//g, '')\n  .split('\\n')\n  .filter((line) => !/^\\s*\\/\\//.test(line))\n  .join('\\n')\n\ntest('the engine imports the hash-fail tracker', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*createHashFailTracker[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/hashfail'/\n    .test(code), 'createHashFailTracker comes from hashfail.js')\n  // A local copy of the regex here would keep matching after a\n  // WebTorrent upgrade reworded the message, so the parser has to be\n  // the one the tests pin.\n  t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*parseFailedPiece[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/hashfail'/\n    .test(code), 'and so does the warning parser')\n  t.ok(/createHashFailTracker\\(/.test(code), 'and the tracker is built')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('block senders are recorded', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/torrent\\.on\\('wire'/.test(code),\n    'the engine subscribes to new wires')\n  t.ok(/wire\\.on\\('piece'/.test(code),\n    \"and to each wire's piece event, which names the sender\")\n  t.ok(/recordContribution\\(\\s*index\\s*,\\s*wire\\.remoteAddress\\s*\\)/\n    .test(code), 'attributing the block to that peer')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('a failed piece is charged to its contributors', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/parseFailedPiece\\(/.test(code),\n    'verification failures are picked out of the warning channel')\n  t.ok(/recordFailure\\(\\s*pieceIndex\\s*\\)/.test(code),\n    'and charged to whoever fed that piece')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// Three parts, and the ban leaks without all three: `client.blocked`\n// stops a banned peer being re-added through discovery, destroying its\n// wires ends the connection it is already on, and the guard on the\n// `wire` event catches everything else.\ntest('a banned peer is refused and disconnected', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/client\\.blocked = \\{[^}]*isBanned\\(host\\)/.test(code),\n    'client.blocked is a live view of the ban set')\n  t.ok(/wire\\.destroy\\(\\)/.test(code),\n    'and live wires to banned peers are destroyed')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// The regression this exists for, measured rather than reasoned about:\n// with the two checks above and no guard on the `wire` event, a\n// localhost seeder serving one corrupt piece was banned and then\n// straight back on the wire, and the leecher logged 123,173 failed\n// verifications in 30 seconds -- against 3 with the guard in place.\n//\n// `client.blocked` is consulted by the public `addPeer` only.\n// WebTorrent's own backoff reconnect in `conn.on('close')` calls the\n// private `_addPeer`, and `_addIncomingPeer` takes incoming\n// connections; neither looks at the blocklist. `Torrent#_onWire` is\n// the one funnel all of them pass through.\ntest('a banned peer cannot get a wire by any other route', function (t) {\n  const handler = /torrent\\.on\\('wire',[\\s\\S]*?\\n {2}\\}\\)/.exec(code)\n  t.ok(handler, 'the wire handler is found')\n\n  const body = handler ? handler[0] : ''\n  t.ok(/isBanned\\(wire\\.remoteAddress\\)/.test(body),\n    'the handler checks the ban before anything else')\n  const guard = /isBanned\\(wire\\.remoteAddress\\)\\) return wire\\.destroy\\(\\)/\n  t.ok(guard.test(body),\n    'and drops the wire instead of listening to it')\n\n  // Order matters: recording blocks from a peer already banned would\n  // let it keep earning strikes it can no longer be punished for, and\n  // means the wire was live long enough to send them.\n  const banAt = body.search(/isBanned\\(wire\\.remoteAddress\\)/)\n  const pieceAt = body.search(/wire\\.on\\('piece'/)\n  t.ok(banAt >= 0 && pieceAt >= 0 && banAt < pieceAt,\n    'the ban check comes before the piece subscription')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// The residue of the guard above. It stops the banned peer's data but\n// not its reconnect: `wire.destroy()` closes the connection, WebTorrent\n// re-adds the peer from `conn.on('close')` through the private\n// `_addPeer`, and `Peer#onHandshake` has already reset `retries` to 0\n// by the time our guard runs -- so the backoff never exhausts. The peer\n// came back every second forever, paying for a handshake and a full\n// bitfield each time, which is the download speed a stalled torrent was\n// showing next to \"corrupt peer blocked\". `_addPeer` is the one funnel\n// both the public `addPeer` and the reconnect pass through.\ntest('a banned peer cannot be re-added by the backoff reconnect',\n  function (t) {\n    t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*guardPeerAdds[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/hashfail'/\n      .test(code), 'the peer-add guard comes from hashfail.js')\n    t.ok(/guardPeerAdds\\(\\s*torrent\\s*,\\s*hashFails\\.isBanned\\s*\\)/\n      .test(code), 'and is installed on every torrent')\n\n    // Installed with the rest of the per-torrent wiring, so a torrent\n    // cannot exist unguarded.\n    const wiring = /function addTorrentEvents \\(torrent\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n\\}/\n      .exec(code)\n    t.ok(wiring, 'addTorrentEvents is found')\n    t.ok(wiring && /guardPeerAdds\\(/.test(wiring[0]),\n      'and the guard goes on with the event handlers')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\n// The direction neither of the guards above covers. `_addPeer` is only\n// reached by peers we add; a peer that dials us arrives through\n// `conn-pool.js:_onConnection` and is handed straight to\n// `_addIncomingPeer`, which consults no blocklist. The `wire` guard\n// still cuts the connection, but `_registerPeer` has by then attached\n// the `download`/`upload` listeners feeding `torrent.downloadSpeed`, so\n// the banned peer's handshake was counted against the torrent -- and it\n// could dial again immediately, for as long as it liked.\ntest('a banned peer cannot get in by dialling us', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*guardIncomingPeers[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/hashfail'/\n    .test(code), 'the incoming guard comes from hashfail.js')\n  t.ok(/guardIncomingPeers\\(\\s*torrent\\s*,\\s*hashFails\\.isBanned\\s*\\)/\n    .test(code), 'and is installed on every torrent')\n\n  const wiring = /function addTorrentEvents \\(torrent\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n\\}/\n    .exec(code)\n  t.ok(wiring && /guardIncomingPeers\\(/.test(wiring[0]),\n    'alongside the outbound guard, so neither direction is left open')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('warnings that are not verification failures still reach the bus',\n  function (t) {\n    // The handler grew a side effect; the passthrough it wrapped has to\n    // survive it, or every tracker warning stops reaching the UI.\n    t.ok(/bus\\.send\\('wt-warning', torrent\\.key, err\\.message\\)/.test(code),\n      'the torrent warning passthrough is intact')\n    t.ok(/bus\\.send\\('wt-warning', null, err\\.message\\)/.test(code),\n      'and so is the client-level one')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('the UI is told about corrupt peers', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/hashFails: corrupt/.test(code),\n    'wt-progress carries the failure count')\n  t.ok(/bannedPeers: corrupt/.test(code),\n    'and the running ban tally')\n  t.ok(/corruptPeerStats\\.delete\\(infoHash\\)/.test(code),\n    'and the tally is dropped with the torrent')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// The tally above only ever goes up, so \"this torrent has banned\n// someone\" stays true forever and cannot answer \"is that why it is\n// stuck now\". The status line needs the second question, so the engine\n// -- which owns the clock -- answers it.\ntest('the UI is told whether a ban explains the current stall',\n  function (t) {\n    t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*bannedSinceProgress[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/hashfail'/\n      .test(code), 'the comparison comes from hashfail.js')\n    t.ok(/lastBanAt/.test(code), 'the engine records when it last banned')\n    t.ok(/bannedSinceProgress: /.test(code),\n      'and wt-progress carries the answer the status line reads')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\n// The ETA and the stall label are only as honest as what they are fed,\n// and they are not fed the same thing. The speed an estimate is built\n// on has to be verified bytes: `torrent.downloaded` includes piece\n// buffers that have not passed their hash, and dividing by that is the\n// original bug in full. The stall cannot use verified bytes, because\n// pieces land minutes apart on a slow torrent and it reported healthy\n// downloads as stuck. `stall.js` takes both and says which is which.\ntest('progress is sampled in verified bytes and payload bytes',\n  function (t) {\n    t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*createStallTracker[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/stall'/\n      .test(code), 'the stall tracker comes from stall.js')\n    t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*verifiedBytes[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/stall'/.test(code),\n      'and so does the verified-byte count')\n\n    const call = /stalls\\.sample\\([\\s\\S]*?\\}, now\\)/.exec(code)\n    t.ok(call, 'the tracker is sampled')\n    const body = call ? call[0] : ''\n    t.ok(/torrent\\.infoHash/.test(body), 'per torrent')\n    t.ok(/verified:\\s*verifiedBytes\\(torrent\\)/.test(body),\n      'the speed is measured in bytes that passed their hash')\n    t.ok(/payload:\\s*downloaded/.test(body),\n      'and the stall in bytes held, which moves per block')\n\n    t.ok(/stalls\\.forget\\(infoHash\\)/.test(code),\n      'and the history is dropped with the torrent')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('the UI is told the verified speed and the stall', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/verifiedSpeed: Math\\.round\\(motion\\.verifiedSpeed\\)/.test(code),\n    'wt-progress carries the speed the ETA is derived from')\n  t.ok(/stalled: motion\\.stalled/.test(code),\n    'and whether the torrent has stopped verifying')\n  // Sending the raw millisecond counter would change the payload on\n  // every tick, defeating the diff above it and putting the whole\n  // object on the bus once a second for every idle torrent.\n  t.ok(!/stalledForMs:/.test(code),\n    'but not the counter that changes every tick')\n  t.end()\n})\nconst test = require('tape')\n\n// Progress measured in verified bytes, the stall measured in payload\n// bytes, and the reasons they are not the same counter. The clock is a\n// parameter, like the sweep's in `announce.js`, so none of this waits\n// on real time.\nconst {\n  createStallTracker, verifiedBytes, STALL_AFTER_MS, SPEED_WINDOW_MS\n} = require('../../build/engine/stall')\n\nconst KEY = 'ccf09fcb'\nconst MB = 1024 * 1024\n\n// Both counters at the same figure, for the cases where the distinction\n// between them is not what is under test.\nconst both = (bytes) => ({ verified: bytes, payload: bytes })\n\ntest('the first sample has nothing to compare against', function (t) {\n  const tracker = createStallTracker()\n  const first = tracker.sample(KEY, both(100 * MB), 1000)\n\n  t.equal(first.verifiedSpeed, 0, 'no speed can be derived from one point')\n  t.equal(first.stalledForMs, 0, 'and nothing has stalled yet')\n  t.equal(first.stalled, false, 'so it is not reported as stalled')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('speed is verified bytes over elapsed time', function (t) {\n  const tracker = createStallTracker()\n  tracker.sample(KEY, both(0), 0)\n  const result = tracker.sample(KEY, both(10 * MB), 10000)\n\n  t.equal(result.verifiedSpeed, MB, '10 MB verified over 10s is 1 MB/s')\n  t.equal(result.stalled, false, 'and it is plainly moving')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// The bug this whole module exists for. A peer that sends handshakes,\n// keepalives and `have` messages but never a usable block keeps\n// `torrent.downloadSpeed` off zero, because that counts every byte off\n11:    \"build\": \"npm run build:node && npm run build:renderer\",\n12:    \"build:node\": \"babel src --out-dir build --delete-dir-on-start --ignore \\\"src/renderer/main.js,src/renderer/components/**/*,src/renderer/controllers/**/*,src/renderer/pages/**/*\\\"\",\n21:    \"test\": \"npm run lint && depcheck --ignores=standard,eslint,@babel/eslint-parser,madr,buffer,nightralph --ignore-dirs=build,build-vite,build-tauri,build-sidecar,dist && npm run test-unit\",\n22:    \"test-unit\": \"npm run build && tape test/unit/*.js\",\n23:    \"lint\": \"standard && npm run lint:boundary\",\n24:    \"lint:boundary\": \"eslint --no-eslintrc -c eslint-boundary.config.js src/renderer src/engine\",\n27:    \"preversion\": \"npm run lint\",\n79:    \"@babel/eslint-parser\": \"7.29.7\",\n84:    \"eslint\": \"8.15.0\",\n90:    \"standard\": \"17.0.0\",\n114:  \"standard\": {\n115:    \"parser\": \"@babel/eslint-parser\"","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14673048-cc01-4d4d-9fd7-2a2cca52022a","uuid":"ebe2b7fb-98e0-41ec-9cb0-20ff4e5b2be5","timestamp":"2026-10-18T01:09:54.172Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# src/engine/\n\nThe torrent engine and its transport (ADR-012). Phase 3 moves the\nengine here from `src/renderer/webtorrent.js` and turns it into a Node\nsidecar; US-039 landed the transport seam first and US-040 added the\nsidecar entry point.\n\n**There is exactly one engine.** US-044 moved the UI onto the sidecar\nand US-045 deleted the hidden window, the engine that ran in it and the\n`ipcMain` relay between them. Engine behaviour has a single home now:\n`src/engine/index.js`.\n\n`build:main` compiles the whole of `src` except the four Vite-owned\npaths, so a file added here is emitted to `build/engine/` with no build\nchange -- that is also the path unit tests require.\n\n## The bus contract\n\nThe engine talks to the UI through an injected bus and never names a\ntransport API itself. A bus is exactly two functions:\n\n- `send(channel, ...args)` -- deliver one message to the UI.\n- `on(channel, handler)` -- subscribe; resolves to an unsubscribe\n  function. The handler receives the protocol's argument list only,\n  never the transport's own event object. Stripping that object is the\n  bus's job, not the caller's.\n\nThe bus carries no protocol. Channel names, argument lists and payload\nshapes live in the command and event tables in\n`docs/platform-shim.md`; the codec that turns them into bytes is\n`src/shared/wire.js`. A bus implementation that knows a channel name is\na bug.\n\nThe engine constructs its bus in exactly one place and\n`test/unit/engine-entry.js` asserts that, by scanning the engine source\nfor transport APIs, for stray `.send(` calls and for subscriptions that\nbypass `bus.on`. That scan is what keeps the seam substitutable, so keep\nit passing rather than relaxing it.\n\n`bus-electron.js`, the Phase 3 transitional implementation, is gone:\nUS-044 pointed the UI at `bus-websocket.js` and US-045 deleted the\nhidden window it depended on, along with its unit test.\n\nThis directory is outside `src/renderer/`, so the renderer's Node bans\ndo not apply: the sidecar is a Node process and uses `fs`, `crypto`\nand friends directly. Since US-046 both boundary mechanisms cover it\nfor the **opposite** mistake instead. `eslint-boundary.config.js` has\nan `src/engine/**` override banning `electron`, `@electron/remote` and\n`original-fs` plus every DOM global, and `npm run lint:boundary` lints\nthis tree as well as the renderer;\n`test/unit/platform-boundary.js` scans the same two things in source,\nwhich is what catches the `require()` spellings ESLint cannot see.\n\n## `bus-websocket.js`\n\nThe sidecar implementation, and the one that survives Phase 4. It binds\na `ws` server to `127.0.0.1` on port 0, encodes every frame with\n`src/shared/wire.js`, and exposes `send`/`on` plus three lifecycle\nfunctions the entry point needs: `listen()` (resolves to the bound\nport, idempotent), `address()` and `close()`.\n\nThree behaviours are contract, not incidental:\n\n- **A send with nobody connected is dropped, not queued.** Commands\n  always originate from the UI, so a connected client is the normal\n  case; the pre-connect queue that ADR-012 requires lives on the *UI*\n  side, in `ipc-engine.js`, and has since US-044.\n- **A malformed frame and a throwing handler are both reported through\n  `onError`, never rethrown and never fatal to the socket.** The engine\n  is a long-lived process; one bad message from a buggy UI must not end\n  the session. `index.js` wires `onError` to the same\n  `wt-uncaught-error` path the process handlers use.\n- **The token, when configured, is checked in `verifyClient`**, which\n  answers a bad upgrade with HTTP 401 before any frame is read. It is\n  read from `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_TOKEN` by `index.js`. No token means an\n  open server, which is only acceptable for running the sidecar by\n  hand. The `Origin` check ADR-012 also asks for is still not\n  implemented -- US-044 connected the UI without needing it, so it needs\n  the real header the Electron and Tauri webviews send and is US-046\n  territory.\n\n## `index.js`\n\nThe sidecar entry point: `node build/engine/index.js`. It imports no\nElectron module and uses no DOM.\n\n**The stdout contract with the host.** Exactly one line matches\n`WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PORT=<port>`, printed once the server is listening.\nThe host end is `src-tauri/src/engine.rs`, which holds the same prefix\nin `PORT_LINE_PREFIX`; keep the prefix and the `=` if you touch either\nside. Every other line on stdout and stderr is pumped through to the\nhost's own output under an `Engine: ` prefix. `src/main/` is gone, so\nthe Electron reader that older plans name here\n(`src/main/engine-process.js`, US-041) went with it.\n\nDifferences from the hidden-window engine, all deliberate:\n\n- No `ipcReadyWebTorrent`. Readiness is the port line plus an accepted\n  socket.\n- No `wt-generate-torrent-poster` and no `wt-poster`. Both needed a DOM;\n  US-043 deleted them from the wire and poster generation now runs in the\n  UI renderer (`src/renderer/lib/torrent-poster.js`, ADR-013). The engine\n  handles no media at all -- do not add a media dependency back to it.\n- No `window.client` and no `window.testOfflineMode`.\n- Uncaught errors come from `process.on('uncaughtException')` and\n  `process.on('unhandledRejection')` rather than a `window` error\n  listener, and still go out on `wt-uncaught-error`.\n- `SIGTERM` and `SIGINT` destroy the client and close every streaming\n  server, so a quit does not leave the download directory held open.\n- With `WEBTORRENT_ENGINE_PARENT_PIPE` set, stdin ending shuts the\n  process down the same way. That is the orphan backstop for a host\n  that dies without running its `before-quit`, and it is gated behind\n  the flag because a hand-run `node build/engine/index.js` gets\n  `/dev/null` on stdin, which ends immediately.\n- `getTorrent()` throws a plain `Error` with the same message\n  `TorrentKeyNotFoundError` produced. The class lives in\n  `src/renderer/lib/errors.js`, only its message ever crossed the wire,\n  and the sidecar does not import renderer code.\n\nThe streaming servers are a `Map` keyed by info hash, and each entry\ncounts its holders (US-043): `wt-start-server` is idempotent per torrent\nand always replies, `wt-stop-server` takes the info hash to release, and\nthe server dies with its last holder. Poster generation and playback can\nhold servers for different torrents at once, so a single module-level\n`server` is not enough any more.\n\n`config.TORRENT_PATH` and `config.POSTER_PATH` resolve under plain\n`node` because US-040 made `src/config.js` require `electron` lazily,\ninside the main-process branch of `getPath()`. Do not hoist that back\nto a top-level import: the packaged sidecar has no\n`node_modules/electron` to resolve.\n\nThe host runs the sidecar as **Electron in Node mode**\n(`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1`), not as a separate `node` binary, so that the\npackaged app needs no second runtime. In that mode\n`process.versions.electron` is set but `process.type` is not and\n`require('electron')` has no `app`, which is why `getPath()` in\n`src/config.js` branches on `process.type` and treats anything that is\nneither `'renderer'` nor `'browser'` as a plain Node process. A check\nfor \"is Electron present\" there breaks the sidecar at load, and only\noutside `NODE_ENV=test` (portable config skips the branch).\n\n## `announce.js`\n\n`announceNow(torrent, dht)` is the discovery half of \"destroy and\nre-add\": a tracker `update()` plus a DHT `lookup()`, with no re-verify\nof pieces on disk and no dropped peer connections. It exists because\nthe natural cadence is slow -- 30 minutes per tracker, 15 minutes plus\njitter for the DHT -- so a torrent whose last peer drops can sit at\nzero peers for half an hour.\n\nThe sweep automates it. Once a minute `index.js` calls\n`runAnnounceSweep`, which re-announces every torrent that has zero\npeers, is not finished and is not paused. Three guards keep it from\nbeing worse than the bug it fixes, and they are the reason the logic\nlives here rather than inline in `index.js`, where it could not be\ntested: a five minute per-torrent cooldown, comfortably above any\ntracker's `min interval`; a cap of five torrents per sweep, served\nleast-recently-announced first so a long list goes round robin; and one\nsummary line per sweep that did anything, carrying the deferred count,\nbecause a silent truncation reads as \"nothing else needed it\".\n\nBe precise about that last one, because the obvious reading is too\nnarrow. The line is not reserved for capped sweeps: a sweep that\nannounces one torrent and defers nothing still logs. What is silent is\na sweep with nothing to do -- `runAnnounceSweep` returns early only\nwhen it selected nothing *and* deferred nothing, so an idle engine\nsays nothing at all.\n\n`index.js` owns only the timer and the `lastAnnounceAt` map. The map\nis seeded when a torrent's info hash arrives -- `client.add()` has just\nannounced `started` and a second announce a minute later would be waste\n-- and cleared in `stopTorrenting`.\n\nThat covers the `wt-stop-torrenting` path, which is the only caller.\nA torrent torn down any other way -- a fatal `torrent` error, say --\nleaves its entry behind. Those orphans are inert rather than a leak:\nthe sweep iterates `client.torrents` and never the map, so an entry\nwith no torrent is never read, and re-adding the same info hash\noverwrites it.\n\nThe three tunables are exported from here, not from `index.js`:\n`SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS`, `COOLDOWN_MS` and `MAX_PER_SWEEP`. `index.js`\nimports the interval rather than repeating `60 * 1000`, so the cadence\nhas one home. `selectStalled` is exported next to `runAnnounceSweep`\nbecause the guards are worth testing without announcing anything.\n\n`test/unit/engine-sweep-wiring.js` pins that wiring by scanning the\n`index.js` source: both imports, `setInterval(runSweep,\nSWEEP_INTERVAL_MS)`, and the seed and the delete on `lastAnnounceAt`.\nAn interval that fires once a minute is too slow to drive from a\nspawned sidecar, and a test-only override of it would be production\ncode that exists for a test -- so renaming `runSweep` or inlining the\nconstant fails that scan instead of going unnoticed.\n  bus.send('wt-server-' + infoHash, info)\n}\n\nfunction stopServer (infoHash) {\n  const entry = servers.get(infoHash)\n  if (!entry) return\n  if (--entry.holders > 0) return\n  servers.delete(infoHash)\n  entry.server.destroy()\n}\n\nfunction stopAllServers () {\n  for (const entry of servers.values()) entry.server.destroy()\n  servers.clear()\n}\n\nconsole.log('Initializing...')\n\nfunction getAudioMetadata (infoHash, index) {\n  const torrent = client.get(infoHash)\n  const file = torrent.files[index]\n\n  // Set initial matadata to display the filename first.\n  const metadata = { title: file.name }\n  bus.send('wt-audio-metadata', infoHash, index, metadata)\n\n  const options = {\n    native: false,\n    skipCovers: true,\n    fileSize: file.length,\n    observer: () => {\n      bus.send('wt-audio-metadata', infoHash, index, {\n        common: metadata.common,\n        format: metadata.format\n      })\n    }\n  }\n  const onMetadata = file.done\n    // If completed; use direct file access\n    ? mm.parseFile(path.join(torrent.path, file.path), options)\n    // otherwise stream\n    : mm.parseStream(file.createReadStream(), file.name, options)\n\n  onMetadata\n    .then(\n      metadata => {\n        bus.send('wt-audio-metadata', infoHash, index, metadata)\n        console.log(`metadata for file='${file.name}' completed.`)\n      },\n      err => {\n        console.log(\n          `error getting audio metadata for ${infoHash}:${index}`,\n          err\n        )\n      }\n    )\n}\n\nfunction selectFiles (torrentOrInfoHash, selections) {\n  // Get the torrent object\n  let torrent\nconst test = require('tape')\nconst fs = require('fs')\nconst path = require('path')\n\n// The banning policy lives in `hashfail.js` and is tested against plain\n// values in `engine-hashfail.js`. What cannot be reached that way is the\n// wiring in `index.js`: driving it for real needs a peer willing to\n// serve corrupt data. So this is a source check, in the same shape as\n// `engine-sweep-wiring.js` -- it asserts the parts are connected, not\n// that they work.\n\nconst ENTRY_SRC = path.join(\n  __dirname, '..', '..', 'src', 'engine', 'index.js')\n\nconst source = fs.readFileSync(ENTRY_SRC, 'utf8')\n\n// Comments discuss all of this at length, so they are stripped first.\n// Every assertion below asserts PRESENCE, which means an unstripped\n// comment body reads as live code and passes -- so block comments are\n// removed whole rather than line by line, for the reason spelled out in\n// `engine-sweep-wiring.js`.\nconst code = source\n  .replace(/\\/\\*[\\s\\S]*?\\*\\//g, '')\n  .split('\\n')\n  .filter((line) => !/^\\s*\\/\\//.test(line))\n  .join('\\n')\n\ntest('the engine imports the hash-fail tracker', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*createHashFailTracker[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/hashfail'/\n    .test(code), 'createHashFailTracker comes from hashfail.js')\n  // A local copy of the regex here would keep matching after a\n  // WebTorrent upgrade reworded the message, so the parser has to be\n  // the one the tests pin.\n  t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*parseFailedPiece[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/hashfail'/\n    .test(code), 'and so does the warning parser')\n  t.ok(/createHashFailTracker\\(/.test(code), 'and the tracker is built')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('block senders are recorded', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/torrent\\.on\\('wire'/.test(code),\n    'the engine subscribes to new wires')\n  t.ok(/wire\\.on\\('piece'/.test(code),\n    \"and to each wire's piece event, which names the sender\")\n  t.ok(/recordContribution\\(\\s*index\\s*,\\s*wire\\.remoteAddress\\s*\\)/\n    .test(code), 'attributing the block to that peer')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('a failed piece is charged to its contributors', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/parseFailedPiece\\(/.test(code),\n    'verification failures are picked out of the warning channel')\n  t.ok(/recordFailure\\(\\s*pieceIndex\\s*\\)/.test(code),\n    'and charged to whoever fed that piece')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// Three parts, and the ban leaks without all three: `client.blocked`\n// stops a banned peer being re-added through discovery, destroying its\n// wires ends the connection it is already on, and the guard on the\n// `wire` event catches everything else.\ntest('a banned peer is refused and disconnected', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/client\\.blocked = \\{[^}]*isBanned\\(host\\)/.test(code),\n    'client.blocked is a live view of the ban set')\n  t.ok(/wire\\.destroy\\(\\)/.test(code),\n    'and live wires to banned peers are destroyed')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// The regression this exists for, measured rather than reasoned about:\n// with the two checks above and no guard on the `wire` event, a\n// localhost seeder serving one corrupt piece was banned and then\n// straight back on the wire, and the leecher logged 123,173 failed\n// verifications in 30 seconds -- against 3 with the guard in place.\n//\n// `client.blocked` is consulted by the public `addPeer` only.\n// WebTorrent's own backoff reconnect in `conn.on('close')` calls the\n// private `_addPeer`, and `_addIncomingPeer` takes incoming\n// connections; neither looks at the blocklist. `Torrent#_onWire` is\n// the one funnel all of them pass through.\ntest('a banned peer cannot get a wire by any other route', function (t) {\n  const handler = /torrent\\.on\\('wire',[\\s\\S]*?\\n {2}\\}\\)/.exec(code)\n  t.ok(handler, 'the wire handler is found')\n\n  const body = handler ? handler[0] : ''\n  t.ok(/isBanned\\(wire\\.remoteAddress\\)/.test(body),\n    'the handler checks the ban before anything else')\n  const guard = /isBanned\\(wire\\.remoteAddress\\)\\) return wire\\.destroy\\(\\)/\n  t.ok(guard.test(body),\n    'and drops the wire instead of listening to it')\n\n  // Order matters: recording blocks from a peer already banned would\n  // let it keep earning strikes it can no longer be punished for, and\n  // means the wire was live long enough to send them.\n  const banAt = body.search(/isBanned\\(wire\\.remoteAddress\\)/)\n  const pieceAt = body.search(/wire\\.on\\('piece'/)\n  t.ok(banAt >= 0 && pieceAt >= 0 && banAt < pieceAt,\n    'the ban check comes before the piece subscription')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// The residue of the guard above. It stops the banned peer's data but\n// not its reconnect: `wire.destroy()` closes the connection, WebTorrent\n// re-adds the peer from `conn.on('close')` through the private\n// `_addPeer`, and `Peer#onHandshake` has already reset `retries` to 0\n// by the time our guard runs -- so the backoff never exhausts. The peer\n// came back every second forever, paying for a handshake and a full\n// bitfield each time, which is the download speed a stalled torrent was\n// showing next to \"corrupt peer blocked\". `_addPeer` is the one funnel\n// both the public `addPeer` and the reconnect pass through.\ntest('a banned peer cannot be re-added by the backoff reconnect',\n  function (t) {\n    t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*guardPeerAdds[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/hashfail'/\n      .test(code), 'the peer-add guard comes from hashfail.js')\n    t.ok(/guardPeerAdds\\(\\s*torrent\\s*,\\s*hashFails\\.isBanned\\s*\\)/\n      .test(code), 'and is installed on every torrent')\n\n    // Installed with the rest of the per-torrent wiring, so a torrent\n    // cannot exist unguarded.\n    const wiring = /function addTorrentEvents \\(torrent\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n\\}/\n      .exec(code)\n    t.ok(wiring, 'addTorrentEvents is found')\n    t.ok(wiring && /guardPeerAdds\\(/.test(wiring[0]),\n      'and the guard goes on with the event handlers')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\n// The direction neither of the guards above covers. `_addPeer` is only\n// reached by peers we add; a peer that dials us arrives through\n// `conn-pool.js:_onConnection` and is handed straight to\n// `_addIncomingPeer`, which consults no blocklist. The `wire` guard\n// still cuts the connection, but `_registerPeer` has by then attached\n// the `download`/`upload` listeners feeding `torrent.downloadSpeed`, so\n// the banned peer's handshake was counted against the torrent -- and it\n// could dial again immediately, for as long as it liked.\ntest('a banned peer cannot get in by dialling us', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*guardIncomingPeers[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/hashfail'/\n    .test(code), 'the incoming guard comes from hashfail.js')\n  t.ok(/guardIncomingPeers\\(\\s*torrent\\s*,\\s*hashFails\\.isBanned\\s*\\)/\n    .test(code), 'and is installed on every torrent')\n\n  const wiring = /function addTorrentEvents \\(torrent\\) \\{[\\s\\S]*?\\n\\}/\n    .exec(code)\n  t.ok(wiring && /guardIncomingPeers\\(/.test(wiring[0]),\n    'alongside the outbound guard, so neither direction is left open')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('warnings that are not verification failures still reach the bus',\n  function (t) {\n    // The handler grew a side effect; the passthrough it wrapped has to\n    // survive it, or every tracker warning stops reaching the UI.\n    t.ok(/bus\\.send\\('wt-warning', torrent\\.key, err\\.message\\)/.test(code),\n      'the torrent warning passthrough is intact')\n    t.ok(/bus\\.send\\('wt-warning', null, err\\.message\\)/.test(code),\n      'and so is the client-level one')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('the UI is told about corrupt peers', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/hashFails: corrupt/.test(code),\n    'wt-progress carries the failure count')\n  t.ok(/bannedPeers: corrupt/.test(code),\n    'and the running ban tally')\n  t.ok(/corruptPeerStats\\.delete\\(infoHash\\)/.test(code),\n    'and the tally is dropped with the torrent')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// The tally above only ever goes up, so \"this torrent has banned\n// someone\" stays true forever and cannot answer \"is that why it is\n// stuck now\". The status line needs the second question, so the engine\n// -- which owns the clock -- answers it.\ntest('the UI is told whether a ban explains the current stall',\n  function (t) {\n    t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*bannedSinceProgress[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/hashfail'/\n      .test(code), 'the comparison comes from hashfail.js')\n    t.ok(/lastBanAt/.test(code), 'the engine records when it last banned')\n    t.ok(/bannedSinceProgress: /.test(code),\n      'and wt-progress carries the answer the status line reads')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\n// The ETA and the stall label are only as honest as what they are fed,\n// and they are not fed the same thing. The speed an estimate is built\n// on has to be verified bytes: `torrent.downloaded` includes piece\n// buffers that have not passed their hash, and dividing by that is the\n// original bug in full. The stall cannot use verified bytes, because\n// pieces land minutes apart on a slow torrent and it reported healthy\n// downloads as stuck. `stall.js` takes both and says which is which.\ntest('progress is sampled in verified bytes and payload bytes',\n  function (t) {\n    t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*createStallTracker[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/stall'/\n      .test(code), 'the stall tracker comes from stall.js')\n    t.ok(/import \\{[^}]*verifiedBytes[^}]*\\} from '\\.\\/stall'/.test(code),\n      'and so does the verified-byte count')\n\n    const call = /stalls\\.sample\\([\\s\\S]*?\\}, now\\)/.exec(code)\n    t.ok(call, 'the tracker is sampled')\n    const body = call ? call[0] : ''\n    t.ok(/torrent\\.infoHash/.test(body), 'per torrent')\n    t.ok(/verified:\\s*verifiedBytes\\(torrent\\)/.test(body),\n      'the speed is measured in bytes that passed their hash')\n    t.ok(/payload:\\s*downloaded/.test(body),\n      'and the stall in bytes held, which moves per block')\n\n    t.ok(/stalls\\.forget\\(infoHash\\)/.test(code),\n      'and the history is dropped with the torrent')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('the UI is told the verified speed and the stall', function (t) {\n  t.ok(/verifiedSpeed: Math\\.round\\(motion\\.verifiedSpeed\\)/.test(code),\n    'wt-progress carries the speed the ETA is derived from')\n  t.ok(/stalled: motion\\.stalled/.test(code),\n    'and whether the torrent has stopped verifying')\n  // Sending the raw millisecond counter would change the payload on\n  // every tick, defeating the diff above it and putting the whole\n  // object on the bus once a second for every idle torrent.\n  t.ok(!/stalledForMs:/.test(code),\n    'but not the counter that changes every tick')\n  t.end()\n})\nconst test = require('tape')\n\n// Progress measured in verified bytes, the stall measured in payload\n// bytes, and the reasons they are not the same counter. The clock is a\n// parameter, like the sweep's in `announce.js`, so none of this waits\n// on real time.\nconst {\n  createStallTracker, verifiedBytes, STALL_AFTER_MS, SPEED_WINDOW_MS\n} = require('../../build/engine/stall')\n\nconst KEY = 'ccf09fcb'\nconst MB = 1024 * 1024\n\n// Both counters at the same figure, for the cases where the distinction\n// between them is not what is under test.\nconst both = (bytes) => ({ verified: bytes, payload: bytes })\n\ntest('the first sample has nothing to compare against', function (t) {\n  const tracker = createStallTracker()\n  const first = tracker.sample(KEY, both(100 * MB), 1000)\n\n  t.equal(first.verifiedSpeed, 0, 'no speed can be derived from one point')\n  t.equal(first.stalledForMs, 0, 'and nothing has stalled yet')\n  t.equal(first.stalled, false, 'so it is not reported as stalled')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('speed is verified bytes over elapsed time', function (t) {\n  const tracker = createStallTracker()\n  tracker.sample(KEY, both(0), 0)\n  const result = tracker.sample(KEY, both(10 * MB), 10000)\n\n  t.equal(result.verifiedSpeed, MB, '10 MB verified over 10s is 1 MB/s')\n  t.equal(result.stalled, false, 'and it is plainly moving')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// The bug this whole module exists for. A peer that sends handshakes,\n// keepalives and `have` messages but never a usable block keeps\n// `torrent.downloadSpeed` off zero, because that counts every byte off\n11:    \"build\": \"npm run build:node && npm run build:renderer\",\n12:    \"build:node\": \"babel src --out-dir build --delete-dir-on-start --ignore \\\"src/renderer/main.js,src/renderer/components/**/*,src/renderer/controllers/**/*,src/renderer/pages/**/*\\\"\",\n21:    \"test\": \"npm run lint && depcheck --ignores=standard,eslint,@babel/eslint-parser,madr,buffer,nightralph --ignore-dirs=build,build-vite,build-tauri,build-sidecar,dist && npm run test-unit\",\n22:    \"test-unit\": \"npm run build && tape test/unit/*.js\",\n23:    \"lint\": \"standard && npm run lint:boundary\",\n24:   